#  Every checkout of this repository has to reproduce the tree that was tested,
#  on any machine. Without this file it does not: core.autocrlf is true on the
#  machine this is developed on, so git rewrites endings on the way in AND out,
#  and `git add lib/` says so out loud -- "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next
#  time Git touches it". Three things here break when that happens.

#  1. The default. Every text blob in this repository is stored LF (measured:
#     38 of 38), and every file added since the vendoring work is LF on disk
#     too, so LF is what a checkout must produce. `text=auto` rather than a
#     bare `text` on purpose -- eleven tracked files are binaries (tor.exe,
#     lyrebird.exe, VC_redist.x64.exe, five PNGs, two JPGs and a BMP) and an
#     unconditional `text` would run eol conversion over tor.exe.
*                       text=auto eol=lf

#  2. The five documents that predate this work are CRLF in the working tree,
#     and .build/audit-eol.js asserts exactly that -- that each file is
#     internally consistent and that these five are still CRLF. They are stored
#     LF like everything else; `eol=crlf` is what puts them back on disk as
#     CRLF, so a fresh clone passes that audit instead of failing it on the
#     first run, on Windows and on Linux alike. Editing them with a
#     single-line anchor and a multi-line replacement is only safe because the
#     file really is all-CRLF, so this is not cosmetic.
#
#     There were six. globe.html was the v1 globe -- one inline <script> and a
#     second copy of the country table -- and nothing loaded it; it is deleted,
#     not merely unlisted, so no future edit can wire it back in.
main.js                 text eol=crlf
renderer.js             text eol=crlf
globe-controller.js     text eol=crlf
index.html              text eol=crlf
style.css               text eol=crlf
#  Written CRLF to match the document that loads it twice.
vendor-umd-shim.js      text eol=crlf
#  The NSIS installer's licence page (package.json -> build.nsis.license) loads
#  this file into a Windows text control, and those break lines on CRLF -- an
#  LF-only licence risks rendering as one endless line on the page every user
#  has to scroll past before installing, and CRLF costs nothing here.
#
#  The file was named LICENSE until the config and the tree were reconciled.
#  build.nsis.license has always said "LICENSE.txt", and a custom licence path
#  that resolves to nothing is not skipped: app-builder-lib's getResource()
#  throws InvalidConfigurationError, "cannot find specified resource". So
#  `npm run dist` could not have produced an installer at all.
LICENSE.txt             text eol=crlf

#  3. The five vendored assets are pinned by SHA-256 in .build/test-vendor.js.
#     They are the only <script> sources index.html loads besides the app's own
#     files, in a window that runs with nodeIntegration on and contextIsolation
#     off -- so that pin is the thing standing between a swapped or
#     CDN-refreshed bundle and arbitrary code with require() on the user's PC.
#     One eol rewrite on checkout changes those bytes, and a clean clone would
#     then fail its own vendor check with the correct file on disk. `binary`
#     (= -text -diff) also stops a review tool trying to render a 600 KB
#     single-line bundle as a diff.
vendor/*.min.js         binary
vendor/*.jpg            binary
vendor/*.png            binary

#  The Tor bundle ships as-is. geoip/geoip6 are plain text that tor reads with
#  either ending, but they are 3 MB of upstream data with a signature history,
#  so nothing in here is normalised either way.
Tor/**                  -text
